# Codebase context via Scout

Before exploring an unfamiliar repo or when you need to understand the project structure, use scout to get a hierarchical summary instead of reading files individually.

## Commands

- `scout context` - L0 workset (active changes) + L1 repo map (directory summaries). Start here.
- `scout context --full` - also include L2 file cards for related files. Use when you need more detail.
- `scout card <file>` - compact summary of a specific file. Use before reading the full file.
- `scout tree` - directory-level overview of the entire repo.
- `scout ls <dir>` - list all file cards in a directory.

## When to use

- Starting any task: run `scout context` first to understand the repo and what the user is working on.
- Before reading a file: run `scout card <file>` to check if you actually need the full content.
- When asked about repo structure: run `scout tree` instead of `find` or directory listings.

## Setup

If scout is not initialized for the current repo:
```bash
scout init . && scout warm
```

## Key rule

Prefer scout cards over opening files just to understand what they contain. Only read the full file when you need to edit it or see implementation details.
